artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let a social organization be characterized by two scalar indicators taking values in the unit interval: a scaled robustness $\hat{R}\in[0,1]$, which measures the organization's ability to maintain its function under perturbations (with $\hat{R}=1$ meaning maximally robust and $\hat{R}=0$ meaning maximally fragile), and a propensity-to-change $\hat{P}\in[0,1]$, used as an empirical proxy for adaptivity (with $\hat{P}=1$ meaning maximally prone to change and $\hat{P}=0$ meaning maximally inert). Define the resilience of the organization as a function $R:[0,1]\times[0,1]\to[0,1]$, $R=R(\hat{R},\hat{P})$, that combines robustness and adaptivity.

Propose, with justification, an explicit functional form $R(\hat{R},\hat{P})$ that satisfies the following qualitative requirements:
(i) symmetry/duality between robustness and adaptivity in the sense that neither one alone, when maximized, yields maximal resilience;
(ii) $R$ should be small when both $\hat{R}$ and $\hat{P}$ are simultaneously close to $0$ or simultaneously close to $1$ (i.e., pure rigidity or pure volatility both reduce resilience);
(iii) $R$ should attain higher values for intermediate compromises between robustness and adaptivity;
(iv) $R(\hat{R},\hat{P})\in[0,1]$ for all admissible inputs.

Determine whether the bilinear form
$$R(\hat{R},\hat{P}) \;=\; \hat{R}(1-\hat{P}) \;+\; \hat{P}(1-\hat{R})$$
is an appropriate choice and analyze its properties (monotonicities, critical points, level sets), or propose and justify an alternative functional form better capturing the trade-off.

